# Transparency scales: scale_alpha

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_alpha()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_alpha.html), which covers
`scale_alpha_continuous()`, `scale_alpha_binned()`, `scale_alpha_discrete()` and
`scale_alpha_ordinal()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it
is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

An alpha scale maps a column to transparency, a way to fade the less important observations.
`scale_alpha()` is short for `scale_alpha_continuous()`, for a column of numbers;
`scale_alpha_binned()` cuts the numbers into bins first, and `scale_alpha_discrete()` and
`scale_alpha_ordinal()` are for discrete and ordered values. Giggly has
`Giggly.Plot.scale_alpha_continuous/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_alpha_discrete/2`, with their
`range:`, but not ggplot2's short name, `scale_alpha()`, nor binned or ordered alpha scales
(`scale_alpha_binned()`, `scale_alpha_ordinal()`), and by default maps `alpha` to a column as
ggplot2's default scale does, a column of numbers onto 0.1 to 1 and one of text spread from 0.1 to
1, and draws the legend that reads it back; a layer can also set it for every mark, as
`Giggly.Plot.geom_point(alpha: 0.4)` does. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy
for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's
name for it.

## Mapping transparency

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point(aes(alpha = year))

# The default range of 0.1-1.0 leaves all data visible
p
```

Every car in `mpg` is from 1999 or 2008, so its points are drawn at the range's two ends, faint
and opaque; the legend, titled `year`, has a key for each of 2000 to 2008 by 2, each as opaque as
that year would be.

```elixir
plot =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{alpha: :year})

render.(plot)
```

## Invisible points

```r
# Include 0 in the range to make data invisible
p + scale_alpha(range = c(0, 1))
```

`scale_alpha()` is `Giggly.Plot.scale_alpha_continuous/2`. The cars from 1999 are drawn fully
transparent, so only those from 2008 show.

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.scale_alpha_continuous(range: {0, 1})
|> render.()
```

## Titling the legend

```r
# Changing the title
p + scale_alpha("cylinders")
```

R's first argument is the scale's name, which titles its legend; Giggly's is `name:`.

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.scale_alpha_continuous(name: "cylinders")
|> render.()
```
